#!/bin/sh
# WB Starter Pre-push Hook — nothing reaches the public site unnamed (#1076)
#
# John: "just make every fix a part of a release. I want to identify code in
# batches where the local code and .io are in the same batch."
#
# GitHub Pages serves this repo straight from main (build_type "legacy",
# source {branch: main, path: /}). There is no deploy workflow, because
# deploying is not an action anyone takes -- a push to main IS the publish.
# So this hook guards the only moment .io ever changes.
#
# It refuses a push to main whose head commit carries no v* tag. It does NOT
# create the release itself: a pre-push hook runs after the ref list is already
# computed, so a commit made here would not be in the push it was made for.
# `npm run ship` is the one command that does the whole thing.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT THE HOOK #743 DELETED
#
# That one ran `npm version patch` on EVERY COMMIT. It bumped 3.0.36 -> 3.0.60
# in two days, 3.0.35 ended up naming forty commits, and four of those numbers
# never reached main at all. It also became something to route around: 16
# commits in one day used --no-verify purely to dodge it, and every one of them
# skipped the real checks with it.
#
# The lesson taken from that is the reason this hook is shaped the way it is:
#   - it fires per PUSH TO MAIN, not per commit, so the number tracks the thing
#     it names -- one version, one published state
#   - it never modifies the tree, so there is nothing to "get around"
#   - it names the single command that satisfies it, so the easy path and the
#     correct path are the same path
#
# Feature branches are untouched. Push them as often as you like; they are not
# published.

remote_main=0
while read -r _local_ref _local_sha remote_ref _remote_sha; do
  case "$remote_ref" in
    refs/heads/main) remote_main=1 ;;
  esac
done

[ "$remote_main" -eq 1 ] || exit 0

if [ -n "$(git tag --points-at HEAD --list 'v*')" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

pending=$(git rev-list --count "$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match='v*' 2>/dev/null || echo HEAD)"..HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo '?')

cat >&2 <<EOF

PUSH REFUSED - this batch has no version.

  A push to main IS the deploy: GitHub Pages serves main directly, so these
  $pending commit(s) would be live on
  https://cielovistasoftware.github.io/wb-starter/ immediately, all of them
  still declaring the PREVIOUS version number.

  That is what #1076 is: not "no version", but one version string naming
  several different published states, so "is this fix in 4.0.2?" stops having
  an answer.

  Ship it instead, which writes What's New from these commits, runs the release
  gates, bumps, tags, and pushes:

      npm run ship

  Reviewing first:

      npm run ship -- --dry

EOF
exit 1
